  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Tagore is an 80-year-old man with benign prostatic hyperplasia and chronic urinary retention on an indwelling catheter, on tamsulosin and finasteride, who comes for his yearly wellness visit; his previous wellness visit was with his former physician last year. Six weeks ago he chose to defer surgery and to try again without the catheter after three to four months of finasteride. He has had no further infection since the one in March and no fever. The catheter is changed every four weeks by the clinic nurse. He writes most mornings and teaches a small class at home once a week. He has had no falls, emergency visits, or hospital stays this year. He answered the health risk assessment himself. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Visit</td><td>Subsequent annual wellness visit</td></tr><tr><td>Active conditions</td><td>Benign prostatic hyperplasia with chronic urinary retention on a catheter; mild frailty</td></tr><tr><td>Medicines</td><td>Tamsulosin 0.4 mg at bedtime, finasteride 5 mg daily, polyethylene glycol as needed, and his herbal preparation</td></tr><tr><td>Safety events</td><td>No falls, emergency visits, or admissions this year; one catheter infection treated at home in March</td></tr><tr><td>Functional status</td><td>Independent in daily activities; help with travel and heavy housework</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Health risk assessment. Completed by the patient before the visit and reviewed during the visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Finding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>How completed</td><td>Answered by the patient</td></tr><tr><td>Self-rated health</td><td>Fair, in his words</td></tr><tr><td>Psychosocial</td><td>Lives with family and helpers; writes and teaches; feels useful and loved</td></tr><tr><td>Tobacco</td><td>Never used tobacco</td></tr><tr><td>Alcohol</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Physical activity</td><td>Walks on the veranda and in the garden most days, 15 to 20 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>Nutrition</td><td>Small, plain meals; weight stable at 66 kg</td></tr><tr><td>Sleep</td><td>Sleeps through most nights since the catheter</td></tr><tr><td>Activities of daily living</td><td>Independent in bathing, dressing, toileting with the catheter, and eating</td></tr><tr><td>Instrumental activities of daily living</td><td>Family manage finances and travel; he manages his own medicines</td></tr><tr><td>Home safety</td><td>Night lights; a rail by the bed; no loose rugs; no firearms</td></tr><tr><td>Falls</td><td>No falls this year</td></tr><tr><td>Hearing and vision</td><td>Hears well in conversation; reads with glasses; eye examination last year</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>No concerns; Mini-Cog normal in March</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

